# Brief: Snake (round 1)

## What the input asks (quoted, `producer`#1)

> Make Snake as a web game played in the browser: the snake moves on a grid and is turned with the arrow keys, eating food makes it one cell longer and adds a point, and running into a wall or itself ends the game. It has a title screen, the score during play, and a game-over screen with the final score and a way to play again.

No other material was supplied.

## Requirements (each is judged at acceptance)

- R1 Web game: it runs in a current desktop browser (Chromium) by opening the delivered `index.html`, or by serving the delivery as static files; no install or build step for the player.
- R2 Grid: the snake moves cell by cell on a visible, bounded grid, continuously, at a steady pace.
- R3 Controls: the arrow keys turn the snake. A key pressing straight back into the snake's own neck must not kill it (ignored). Arrow keys must not scroll the page.
- R4 Food: eating food makes the snake exactly one cell longer and adds exactly one point; new food then appears on a free cell (never on the snake).
- R5 Death: moving into a wall or into the snake's own body ends the game.
- R6 Title screen: shown first, names the game, says how to start and how to play (arrow keys).
- R7 Score during play: the current score is visible the whole time and updates on each food eaten.
- R8 Game-over screen: shows the final score and offers a way to play again (key and/or button, stated on screen); playing again starts a fresh game (score 0, initial length) without reloading the page.
- R9 Look and feel: snake, food, grid/walls and screens are visually distinct, readable and consistent in style. Sound is not asked; if present it must not be broken or jarring.

## Focus and priorities for this round

1. Correct core loop (R2–R5) first: this is the game.
2. The three screens and score (R6–R8), with a working replay.
3. Art and polish (R9) on top of a correct game; never at the cost of 1 and 2.
4. No console errors during a full play (title → play → eat → die → replay).

## Standard of acceptance

The producer approves only a delivery that: was played by the producer in a headless browser through title → play → eating several foods → death by wall and by self → replay; was checked by `qa` against R1–R9 with reproducible reports; and was played and reported on by `playtester` on that same build. Open defects against R1–R8 block release.

## Not established by the input (left to the owners, within the standard above)

Grid size, speed, starting length and position, whether speed rises with score, high-score keeping, sound, touch/mobile controls. Owners decide; none of these is required.

## Split (see `assignment.json`)

- `game-developer`: the game (repository root of the delivery, entry `index.html`) and builds to play.
- `artist`: assets under `assets/` in the delivery, matched to what the game loads (agree names/sizes with `game-developer`).
- `qa`: checks of each build against R1–R9, defect reports in the owner's repository; no delivered path.
- `playtester`: plays each build as a player, reports with evidence (captures); no delivered path.
- `producer`: this brief, the assignment, acceptance and approval.
